                "text": "The rule seems to be about flood-filling from the border: any 0-cell reachable from the edge becomes 3, while fully enclosed pockets that can't reach the border become 2. I need to apply this flood-fill logic to the test grid now.\n\nI'm tracing wall positions row by row to map out which cells are walls (4) and which are open, then working through the flood fill from the border cells inward, checking connectivity through gaps in the walls to determine which interior pockets get cut off and marked as enclosed.\n\nThat confirms it's enclosed, so that region counts as 2.\n\nNow checking the right region cols14-19: this section connects through gaps at r8 and continues downward through r13's opening at cols15-19, eventually reaching the bottom border, so it's outside and counts as 3.\n\nLooking at rows14-22: the left columns border out to 3, and cols7-12 connects rightward through the r19 opening to the border, so that's also 3.\n\nSo the only 2-regions are rows3-8 cols3-5 and the rows3-12 cols7-12 area, and I'm now mapping out the exact cell boundaries of that second region row by row.\n\nNow I'm assembling each output row cell by cell, marking wall columns as 4, region cells as 2, and everything else as 3, working through rows0 to row10.",
